# Accepted govulncheck advisories — reachable findings that CANNOT be fixed by
# bumping a dependency. Every entry needs a reason and an exit condition,
# exactly like the carried pins, and the whole file is re-audited on every T2
# upgrade.
#
# Adding an entry here is a last resort: if a fixed release exists, bump the
# module instead.

# golang.org/x/crypto/openpgp is unmaintained BY DESIGN — the advisory is
# permanent, no fixed version will ever exist ("Fixed in: N/A"), and this module
# is already on the latest x/crypto.
#
# THE REACHABILITY PATH HERE IS NOT THE CHAIN'S. The chain accepts the same
# advisory because cosmos-sdk x/upgrade -> hashicorp/go-getter links openpgp for
# detached-signature verification of upgrade artefacts — a path timeflared never
# invokes. That justification does NOT transfer to guardiand, which reaches it by
#
#   blockchain/keys.go -> cosmos-sdk keyring -> openpgp/armor
#
# — the file-backed keyring's ASCII-armour encoding, which guardiand genuinely
# uses to store custody keys. This is a live code path, not dead weight, and it
# is recorded as such rather than inheriting a "never invoked" claim that would
# be false here.
#
# Why it is accepted regardless: the advisory is a maintenance warning about the
# package's status, not a specific exploitable defect, and the armour layer is an
# encoding of material already protected by the keyring passphrase. The exposure
# is what an unmaintained dependency implies for future fixes, not a break today.
#
# Exit: when cosmos-sdk's keyring drops openpgp/armor, or when x/crypto removes
# the package and forces the issue. Re-check on every cosmos-sdk bump — this
# entry is tied to the SDK's keyring implementation, so an SDK upgrade can retire
# it.
